    bbqs:title "Neurobehavioral Mechanisms of Social Behavior and Familial Structure" .

bid:cbc8640e-6d05-4872-bf01-6e3f7d89c349 a bbqs:Project ;
    bbqs:abstract """Project summary
Complex human behavior - such as driving a car - involves the flexible sequencing of many brief tasks in order
to achieve a goal. Current constrained cognitive tasks completely miss this “task control” aspect of cognition,
leaving us with little understanding of the underlying mechanisms. The behavior of subjects with lesions in the
frontal cortex or with neuropsychiatric disease illustrates what is being missed: such patients can often perform
simple behaviors, but fail to properly sequence these behaviors to achieve a goal. Therefore, the fundamental
deficit that these patients suffer from is not visible during simple behaviors, making its study impossible. There
are two major challenges to studying sufficiently complex behavior that involves task control. First one needs to
quantify complex unconstrained behaviors such as social interactions or driving a car. Second, one needs to
simultaneously observe human brain activity at cellular-level resolution. Here, as part of the BRAIN initiatives
Brain and Behavior Quantification and Synchronization consortium, we tackle these challenges by (i)
developing instrumentation to monitor and quantify human behavior in unprecedented detail, and (ii) combining
this new system with large-scale recordings from single neurons in the brains of epilepsy patients. We will
develop a system to acquire and synchronize multi-modal streams of behavioral data, including a head-
mounted eye tracking device to track gaze position, egocentric and allocentric video and audio, and a wearable
device to track biophysiological signals (Aim 1, R61). This data will then be labeled automatically using artificial
intelligence methods, including labeling objects fixated by the subject, transcribing what is being said from
audio, and identifying behavioral states based on the biophysiology (Aim 2). The new system will be validated
for two use cases: ambulatory control subjects and patients in the epilepsy monitoring unit. We will then utilize
the newly developed system to examine the neural mechanisms of task control with synchronized
simultaneous single-neuron recordings in human epilepsy patients (Aim 3). We will identify behavioral and
neural modules (“microtasks”) and the sequence by which they are engaged, and will then examine the
overarching hypothesis that groups of neurons are specialized for executing a specific microtask. These
experiments promise to reveal new insights into the brain architecture of task control that were made possible
by the availability of quantified multi-modal behavior. Lastly, an overarching aim is to construct and train a large
foundation model that learns relationships between the different streams of behavior and the neural recordings.
Our system will be released as open source and requires only commercially available devices and a cellphone,
making the system widely available for others to use in their research. Our multi-modal behavior and
synchronized neural recording approach will lay the groundwork for revealing how the human brain achieves
“task control”, an understanding of which is urgently needed to develop new treatments for the effects of frontal
lobe lesions and psychiatric diseases that affect executive control.""" ;
    bbqs:award_amount 1417650 ;
    bbqs:description """Project summary
Complex human behavior - such as driving a car - involves the flexible sequencing of many brief tasks in order
to achieve a goal. Current constrained cognitive tasks completely miss this “task control” aspect of cognition,
leaving us with little understanding of the underlying mechanisms. The behavior of subjects with lesions in the
frontal cortex or with neuropsychiatric disease illustrates what is being missed: such patients can often perform
simple behaviors, but fail to prope""" ;
    bbqs:fiscal_year 2026 ;
    bbqs:grant_number "R61MH142354" ;
    bbqs:mechanism "R61" ;
    bbqs:name "Multimodal AI-enabled synchronized neural, physiological, and behavioral recordings in humans during unstructured behavior to study dynamic task control" ;
    bbqs:nih_link "https://reporter.nih.gov/project-details/1R61MH142354-01" ;
    bbqs:onboarding_status "not_started" ;
    bbqs:reporter_project_num "1R61MH142354-01" ;
